      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Yes, hail damage is typically covered by standard homeowners insurance policies in Utah.",
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Details on Hail Coverage in Utah"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "What is Covered: Standard policies (usually HO-3 or similar forms) cover sudden and accidental damage to your home\u2019s exterior\u2014such as your roof, siding, windows, and gutters\u2014caused by a hailstorm."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Deductibles: Many Utah insurers have moved to separate, higher percentage or flat deductibles specifically for wind and hail damage (sometimes jumping to $10,000 or a high percentage of your dwelling value on renewal). Check your policy declarations page to see what your hail deductible is.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "RCV vs. ACV:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "RCV (Replacement Cost Value) pays the full cost to repair or replace the damaged items with new material of like kind and quality (minus your deductible)."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "ACV (Actual Cash Value) pays only for the depreciated value of the roof or item based on its age and wear.",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  2
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Wear and Tear Exclusions: Insurers will often try to deny claims by citing \"normal wear and tear\" or pre-existing damage rather than acute storm impact. If this happens, you can request a second inspection, hire a public engineer, or bring in an independent inspector.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Filing Timelines: While some policies state shorter reporting windows, state laws or policy conformance clauses may give you up to three years to file a storm claim, though waiting can hurt your case if the insurer claims \"prejudice\" (lost evidence). It is always best to document and report damage promptly.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "What specific part of your home was damaged (roof, siding, windows)?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Who your current insurance carrier is?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can help you understand what steps to take next or how to prepare for an adjuster visit."
      }
    ],
